// draft
// drafted
type Series = { items: Text[] }
// drafted
type Row = { cells: Option<Text>[] }
// drafted
type ZipOffsetError = { code: Text, display: Text }

// drafted
mismatch_error: ZipOffsetError = {code: "e-offsets-count", display: "Number of iterables and offsets didn't match"}

zip_offset(iterables: Series[], offsets: Int[], longest: Bool, fillvalue: Option<Text>) -> Result<Row[], ZipOffsetError>
	note "zip the iterables together, the i-th shifted by the i-th offset, stopping at the shortest unless longest is set, filling gaps with fillvalue"
	# The output is Ok exactly when there are as many offsets as iterables.

	# Every error output is the mismatch error.

	# Every row has one cell per iterable, in the order of the iterables.

	# Cell i of row k is the item of iterable i at position k plus offset i when that position is
	# within iterable i, and the fillvalue otherwise.

	# There are rows only if there is at least one iterable.

	# If longest is false and the output has rows, every iterable has at least the number of rows
	# plus its offset items.

	# If longest is false and there is at least one iterable, some iterable has at most the number
	# of rows plus its offset items.

	# If longest is true, every iterable has at most the number of rows plus its offset items.

	# If longest is true and the output has rows, some iterable has at least the number of rows plus
	# its offset items.
	// example wanted: iterables "0123" and "abcdef" at offsets 0 and 1, longest false: the first
	// iterable is the shortest once shifted, and it decides the row count
	// example wanted: the same iterables and offsets with longest true: the second iterable decides
	// the row count, and the first runs past its end into the fillvalue
	// example wanted: iterables "ab" and "wxyz" at offsets -2 and 0, longest false: the negative
	// offset puts fillvalue cells at the start, not the end
	// example wanted: iterables "abc" and "wxyz" at offsets 3 and 0, longest false: an offset
	// exactly equal to an iterable's length leaves it nothing, the boundary of the row count
	// example wanted: iterables "abc" and "wxyz" at offsets 5 and 0, longest false: an offset past
	// an iterable's end, where its length minus its offset is below zero
	// example wanted: iterables "ab" and "wxyz" at offsets 0 and 0, longest true, fillvalue "x":
	// the fill is the given value, not None
	// example wanted: no iterables and no offsets, either value of longest: the counts match and
	// there is nothing to zip
	// example wanted: two iterables and one offset: the counts differ, so the error case decides it
